 "issue": 460,
 "issue_title": "GSSoC: mprove user-facing errors for unsupported pandas dtypes",
 "issue_body": "### Track\n\npandas interoperability\n\n### GSSoC level\n\nLevel 1 - beginner\n\n### Context\n\nCurrently, unsupported pandas dtypes (such as datetime64[ns], mixed object, or nested Python objects) may raise generic or unclear exceptions during dataframe conversion or schema inference.\nThis issue aims to improve experience by providing descriptive error messages whenever unsupported dtypes are encountered.\n\n### Scope\n\nHandle unsupported dtypes such as:\n- `datetime64[ns]`\n- mixed `object`\n- nested lists/dicts\n- categorical\n- timedelta\n- complex numbers\n\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- Clear exception messages for unsupported dtypes\n- Column name included in error\n- Suggested fix/conversion included\n- Unit tests added for unsupported dtype categories\n\n### Notes for contributors\n\n_No response_",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/460",
 "pr": 506,
 "pr_title": "git push origin fix/pandas-dtype-errorsfix: clear errors for unsupported pandas dtypes in from_pandas()",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\nImproved user-facing errors for unsupported pandas dtypes in `from_pandas()`.\r\nPreviously, passing a datetime64, timedelta, categorical, or complex column would\r\nraise a generic or confusing error. Now a clear `TypeError` is raised with the\r\ncolumn name and a suggested fix.\r\n\r\n**Behavior change (intentional):** datetime-like pandas columns (datetime64, timedelta)\r\nwere previously silently converted to strings. This PR changes that to a hard failure\r\nwith a clear error message and fix suggestion. Silent conversions hide data integrity\r\nissues from users \u2014 explicit errors are the correct direction.\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\nFixes #460\r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n- [ ] Bug fix\r\n- [x] Feature\r\n- [ ] Performance improvement\r\n- [ ] Documentation\r\n- [x] Tests\r\n- [ ] Refactor\r\n- [ ] CI / packaging\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n- [ ] CSV parser / I/O\r\n- [ ] C++ cleaning engine\r\n- [ ] Python API / ArFrame\r\n- [ ] Pipeline / custom steps\r\n- [ ] Data quality / profiling\r\n- [ ] Schema validation\r\n- [x] pandas interoperability\r\n- [ ] Docs / website\r\n- [ ] Developer tooling\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n- [x] Other: `pytest tests/test_convert.py -v` \u2192 43 passed, 0 failed\r\n\r\n## Screenshots / Media\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Performance Impact\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n- [x] I read the contributing guide.\r\n- [x] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n- [x] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\r\n- [ ] I added or updated docs/examples for public API changes.\r\n- [x] I checked that generated files, logs, and local build artifacts are not committed.\r\n- [x] My PR title uses Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`).\r\n\r\n## Maintainer notes\r\n- Applied `black` formatting to both files\r\n- Fixed trailing whitespace in `tests/test_convert.py`\r\n- Behavior change from silent string conversion to hard failure is intentional \u2014\r\n  surfaces data integrity issues early rather than silently losing type information",
